# Secure files

{{projectName}} was generated with the `secure_files` capability. A stored
file's bytes are exposed only after they were hashed, scanned and released
clean; nothing here can be switched off by a setting. The Starter's
`docs/SECURE_FILES.md` is the full reference; this page is what an operator of
this product needs.

## It refuses to start without

The API and the worker both refuse to start, naming every missing setting, unless
all of these are present and valid. There is no fallback to unscanned release.

| Setting | Requirement |
|---------|-------------|
| `FILE_SCAN_BACKEND` | a real scanner (`clamd`), never `none` |
| `FILE_SCAN_CLAMD_HOST` | set |
| `FILE_SCAN_CLAMD_PORT` | a port, 1-65535 |
| `STORAGE_ENDPOINT` | set |
| `STORAGE_BUCKET` | set |
| `STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY` | set |
| `STORAGE_SECRET_KEY` | set |
| `REDIS_URL` | set: a confirmed upload is finalized by a job |
| `DATABASE_URL` | set: the finalizer works on the file's row, as its tenant |

These have a default that passes, and the worker (and the API) refuse a value outside its range
rather than scan with a limit nobody chose:

| Setting | Default | Range |
|---------|---------|-------|
| `FILE_SCAN_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` | 5 | above 0, at most 60, not above the next |
| `FILE_SCAN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` | 120 | above 0, at most 600 |
| `FILE_SCAN_MAX_BYTES` | 104857600 (100 MiB) | 1 to 104857600: lowered here, never raised, because that is what the clamd service is built for |
| `FILE_SCAN_MAX_ATTEMPTS` | 12 | 1 to 32767 |

Later releases of the capability add rows; the list lives in
`services/api/koras_api/core/secure_files.py` (`SECURE_FILES_CHECKS`). There is no setting, in
any environment, that makes `FILE_SCAN_BACKEND=none` acceptable.

## Uploads

A ticket is issued only for a SHA-256 claim (`checksum_sha256`, 64 lowercase hex
characters, required), and it lets the browser write one *incoming* key and nothing
else. The worker's `file.finalize` job copies the object to a *final* key only it can
write, after checking the object's provenance and the digest of both the incoming and
the final bytes against the claim. Until that has happened the file is on a key a client
could still write, is not downloadable, and is not handed to anything.

If a file stays unavailable: check the worker is running and can reach `REDIS_URL`,
`DATABASE_URL` and the bucket; the sweep (`sweep_finalize`, every five minutes) picks up
anything a lost job left. A file the finalizer refused carries a `scan_failure` word and a
`storage.upload.held` audit event; `integrity_mismatch` means the bytes were not the ones
claimed and is never retried. The bucket's CORS rule must allow the signed upload headers
(see the Starter's `docs/SECURE_FILES.md`).

## The scanner

Once a file is on its final key the worker queues one `file.scan` job for it. The job reads the
object once, asks the clamd service (`FILE_SCAN_CLAMD_HOST`:`FILE_SCAN_CLAMD_PORT`, the private
address of `services/clamd`) and writes a verdict through the guarded transitions: `clean`,
`infected` (the file is quarantined, one security event) or held, which leaves it `pending` with
a closed `scan_failure` word. A scanner's `OK` is only a candidate: the digest, the object's
identity and, for a ZIP-family file, a structural check must all pass before a file is `clean`.
A held file is retried by `sweep_pending_scans` every five minutes with a back-off that caps at
an hour and never stops; at `FILE_SCAN_MAX_ATTEMPTS` attempts one `storage.object.scan_exhausted`
security event tells a person to look, and the file stays held.

If files stay `pending`: check the clamd service is deployed **in this environment** and its
host and port are what the worker has; check the worker's logs for the word on the file
(`scanner_unavailable` is the service, `integrity_mismatch` is bytes that are not the claim and is
not something a retry can change, `inspection_incomplete` is a container the scanner cannot be trusted to have
inspected, `over_ceiling` is an object larger than the scanner takes). Nothing is released while
it is held.

## What is released, and to whom

One rule (`services/api/koras_api/core/file_release.py`) decides whether a file's bytes may be
handed on, and everything that does so asks it: the download, the assistant's indexer and
retrieval, a stored conversation's cited passages, and an import's source file. Only a file that is
`ready`, `clean`, on a final key of **its own tenant** and stamped by the scanner is released; a
`pending`, `skipped`, `infected`, unrecognised or unstamped file is not, and nothing configurable
changes that. A refusal is recorded once (`storage.object.release_refused`, or
`storage.object.quarantined` for an infected file) with a closed reason and never a file name, a key
or anything the scanner said, and the page shows one neutral sentence.

A file that was clean and stops being so (a late `infected` verdict, an archive, a restore's
replacement) stops being released at once, and in the same database statement what the assistant
derived from it -- its chunks and its index state -- is deleted. `files.indexed_at` is therefore not
a record that a file *was* indexed; it is a statement about now.

If a file the person can see will not download: look at `scan_status` (`pending` means the scanner
has not decided; see the section above) and at `scan_object_etag` (a `clean` file without one was
not marked by the scanner and is not released). Do not edit either by hand: the scanner's
transitions are the only writer of a verdict, and a test fails if anything else gains the ability.

{{#if capability.storage_governance}}
## Restoring a file

A restore never makes a file releasable by itself. An approved restore writes the backup's bytes
to a **new** object, on a final key (`.../final/<generation>/<name>`) nothing ever signed an upload
for, after hashing them against the digest the backup recorded and reading what it wrote back; then
the file's row is pointed at the new object and reset to `pending` with none of the old verdict,
scan evidence or derived content, in one statement. The old object is neither overwritten nor
deleted, so a download link issued before the restore still reads the old bytes and nothing else.
The restored file is released only when the scanner has said `clean` about the new object.

A file that was found infected is not replaced in place: restore it as a new file, which is
scanned on its own. A file that has no verdict yet, or was scanned within the last quarter of an
hour, is refused with a closed reason (see the request's error); ask again once the scan has
finished. Restores run from the worker every ten minutes and a request the worker could not ask
the scanner about is followed up by the same worker; the scan sweep also finds a restored file on
its own, because it is on a final key.

If a restored file stays `pending`, look at it as any other pending file (above). The old object
stays in the bucket until somebody removes it: nothing here deletes it.

{{/if}}## Changing the mode

`secure_files` is a generation-time choice. It is not a runtime toggle and is not
retrofitted by upgrading the Starter. Do not hand-edit it in or out: regenerate,
or extend the profile manifest in `koras-saas-starter`.
